1.bug_scenario:
In Perl code the “m” regex match operator with custom delimiters (e.g. m(/$/)) isn’t being recognized correctly by the highlighter. In the snippet

    $d .= '/' if $d !~ m(/$/);

only the literal "/" before the dollar‐anchor is highlighted as a regex, and the rest is treated as plain text. The parser currently only looks for “/…/” after the “m” or “qr” operator, so constructs like m|/$|, m#…#, m{…}, or qr(/$/) are not detected as regexes.
